# ===== PRE-REGISTRATION, before any data, 2026-09-12 =====
# CAN A MODEL STILL PICK THE RIGHT SKILL FROM A SHORTENED CATALOGUE?
#
# ADR-0155's second addendum prices three catalogue shapes and recommends "name + first sentence"
# (66% cheaper, 24% of a 32k window against today's 69%). It also asserts that names alone are
# "probably too cryptic to choose from" and flags that sentence, in the ADR itself, as the one claim
# that is a judgement rather than a measurement. This measures it.
#
# WHY IT MATTERS BEFORE ANY CODE. A cheaper catalogue that the model cannot choose from is not a
# saving, it is a capability regression paid for in tokens. The whole reason the catalogue looked
# like a free win was that it is a LOOKUP -- and a lookup that stops working is not free.
#
# DESIGN. For a sample of skills, construct a query from the skill's BODY (not its description),
# present the catalogue in three shapes, and ask the model to name the single best skill.
#   arm FULL   : name + full description       (today: ~22,759 tok)
#   arm FIRST  : name + first sentence         (~7,722 tok)
#   arm NAMES  : name only                     (~659 tok)
# The query is drawn from the body so that a correct answer cannot come from lexical overlap with
# the very text the shortened arms remove -- otherwise FULL wins by construction and the experiment
# measures nothing but string matching.
#
# PRE-REGISTERED DECISION RULES:
# (1) PRIMARY: accuracy of FIRST against FULL. If FIRST is within a few points of FULL, the
#     recommended shape is safe and the 66% saving is real. If FIRST drops materially, the
#     recommendation in the addendum is WRONG and must be retracted before anything is built.
# (2) SECONDARY: NAMES against FULL. The addendum guesses NAMES is too cryptic. If NAMES holds up,
#     that guess was wrong and the available saving is 97%, not 66% -- a materially better outcome
#     than the one I recommended, which is exactly why the guess had to be tested rather than
#     shipped.
# (3) A CEILING CONTROL IS REQUIRED. If FULL itself scores poorly, the task is too hard or the
#     query construction is broken, and NO arm comparison means anything. FULL is the instrument's
#     own positive control: a low FULL voids the run rather than making the cheap arms look good.
# (4) Ties and refusals count as misses in every arm, scored identically.
# (5) Sample is 30 skills, fixed by a seeded shuffle recorded in the log BEFORE the runs, so the
#     sample cannot be reselected after seeing an arm's score.
[
 "analyze-npc-behavior",
 "analyze-remote-corpus",
 "aspirations",
 "aspirations-complete-review",
 "aspirations-consolidate",
 "aspirations-curate-memory",
 "aspirations-evolve",
 "aspirations-learning-gate",
 "aspirations-precheck",
